And as his bow was drawn without pause, swift-dealing, the twang of its string was heard exceedingly loud, like the crash of the thunderbolt.
Car-warriors and horsemen, elephants, steeds, and foot-soldiers—the fierce shafts loosed by that deft-handed one ground them all to ruin.
Like a thundering rain-cloud, wind-driven at summer's end, raining down a hail of stones, he poured forth terror upon his foes.
Then that lord ranged on, O king, throwing the host into turmoil, and swelled in his enemies a terror more than human.
His bow, adorned with gold, sweeping about upon that chariot-cloud, was seen again and again like lightning amid the storm-clouds.
That hero, truthful, wise, ever devoted to righteousness, yet now most terrible, set surging a dreadful river, like the driver of doom at the ending of an age—
a river born of the rushing torrent of his wrath, thronged with flocks of flesh-eating beasts, brimming on every side with floods of soldiery, sweeping away heroes as its trees;
its water blood, its whirlpools chariots, its banks built of elephants and horses, carrying coats of mail as its rafts, choked with the mire of flesh;
its sands fat and marrow and bone, foaming with fine turbans, filled by the rain-clouds of war, teeming with javelins for fish;
sprung from men and elephants and horses, bearing along its swift flood of arrows, with corpses for its driftwood and water-chestnuts, swarming with arms for its water-snakes;
its pebbled bed strewn with severed heads, haunted by swords for sharks, set with deep pools of chariots and elephants, its lotuses the manifold ornaments;
with eddies of hundreds of great car-warriors, garlanded with waves of the dust of the earth—easy to cross for the mighty in battle, but for the timid impassable;
strewn with heroes for its serpents, frequented by living men for its traders, its great swans the severed parasols, thronged with diadems for its water-birds.

It was strewn full with gold-nocked arrows, awash with floods of blood, and with the gleaming heads of heroes still wearing their earrings;
covered with many-hued saddle-cloths and with banners, with yak-tail fans and elephant-housings, and with scattered raiment of the finest sort;
with the bright ornaments of chariots, horses, men, and elephants, and with sharp, burnished swords cast loose like serpents from their sloughs.
Strewn with bows and broken arrows, with lances, javelins, darts, and battle-staves, and with many other weapons of every kind, the earth shone resplendent.
With lifeless horses, fair of limb and drenched in blood, brought down with their riders by the son of Subhadrā, the ground was made rugged;
with elephants—their goads and their drivers, their armor, weapons, and standards with them—shattered like mountains and crushed by his arrows;
and with foremost chariots strewn upon the ground, their horses, charioteers, and warriors gone, their elephants slain—like lakes whipped into turmoil.
With slain bands of foot-soldiers, adorned with weapons of every kind, the earth became a thing of dreadful aspect, begetting terror in the timid.
Seeing him fallen to the earth, him whose splendor was like the sun and the moon, supreme was the gladness of your men, and grief was upon the Pāṇḍavas.
When Abhimanyu was slain, O king—a child not yet come to his youth—the whole host fled in rout before the very eyes of the King of Righteousness.
Seeing his army breaking apart now that the son of Subhadrā was struck down, Ajātaśatru spoke these words to his heroes:
"This hero, slain with his face never turned away, has gone to heaven. Stand firm; fear not; we shall yet conquer our foes in the field."
Thus that mighty one of great splendor, the King of Righteousness, foremost of warriors, speaking to the grieving, drove away their sorrow.

Behold, O brahmin, with your followers, the fruit of this roaring of mine: having slain the sons of Pāṇḍu in battle, together with Kṛṣṇa and the Sātvatas, I shall give to Duryodhana an earth cleansed of its thorns.
Kṛpa said: I cannot accept this babbling of your daydreams, O son of a sūta, when you would hurl yourself against the two Kṛṣṇas and the Pāṇḍava, the King of Righteousness.
There victory is certain, Karṇa, where stand those two masters of war; even when arrayed for battle, the two Kṛṣṇas and the Pāṇḍava are unconquerable by gods, gandharvas, yakṣas, men, serpents, and rākṣasas.
Devoted to the brahmins, truthful in speech, self-controlled, a worshipper of teachers and gods, ever delighting in dharma, and above all skilled in arms, steadfast and grateful — such is Yudhiṣṭhira, the son of Dharma.
And his brothers are mighty, practised in every weapon, devoted to the service of their elders, wise, ever true to dharma, and renowned.
Their kinsmen too are valiant as Indra, loyal, and skilled in striking — Dhṛṣṭadyumna and Śikhaṇḍin, the son of Durmukha, and Janamejaya;
Candrasena, Bhadrasena, Kīrtidharman, Dhruva, Dhara, Vasucandra, Dāmacandra, Siṃhacandra, and Suvedhana;
and likewise the sons of Drupada, and Drupada himself, knower of the great weapons, for whose cause the king of the Matsyas with his followers stands ready;
Śatānīka, Sudarśana, Śrutānīka, Śrutadhvaja, Balānīka, Jayānīka, Jayāśva, and Rathavāhana;
Candrodaya and Kāmaratha, the noble brothers of Virāṭa, the twins, the sons of Draupadī, and the rākṣasa Ghaṭotkaca — for those in whose cause these fight, there is no destruction.
Truly, if they wished, Bhīma and Phalguna could by the power of their weapons make an utter end of the whole world — with its gods, asuras, and men, its hosts of yakṣas and rākṣasas, its spirits, serpents, and elephants.

the Lord, exceedingly hard to behold even for all the gods, subtler than the subtlest and vaster than the vast,
Rudra the Ruler, the Bull, the all-perceiving, the unborn, the supreme, who whether men move or stand still abides in the heart of every being;
irresistible, hard to look upon, fierce in wrath, great of soul, the destroyer of all, the wise; bearing a divine bow and two quivers, clad in golden armor, of boundless valor;
wielder of the Pināka bow, of the thunderbolt, of the blazing trident, of the battle-axe, of the mace, of the long sword; fair of brow, crowned with the moon amid a circle of matted locks, clad in a tiger's skin, holding a bludgeon and a staff in his hand;
adorned with fair armlets, the sacred thread a serpent across his breast, resplendent with all his hosts, the throngs of spirits; the one made whole, the very repository of austerities, well-praised with chosen words by those who have passed beyond age;
whom water, heaven, the sky, the earth, the moon and sun, and wind and fire, and the measured world itself, cannot suffice to behold — the unborn one, whom those of perverse conduct cannot see, slayer of the foes of Brahman, the source of the deathless;
whom only Brāhmaṇas of holy conduct behold, who, their sin spent, are free of grief in mind. Devoted to him, through austerity and through love of him — that adorable, righteous one of universal form — he beheld him; and seeing him, with speech and mind and intellect and body, his soul thrilling, he rejoiced in the God of gods,
girded with a rosary of beads, the supreme treasure of all lights. Then Nārāyaṇa, beholding him, saluted the source of the universe —
the boon-giver, together with Pārvatī of broad and lovely form, the Lord, the unborn, the Ruler, the unperturbed, the very essence of all causes, the imperishable.

Those who are devoted to the boon-giving god, the auspicious Rudra, the lord of Umā, having won happiness in this world, go to the supreme goal.
Pay homage, O son of Kuntī, to him, ever serene — to Rudra, the white-throated, the youngest, the radiant,
to the one of braided locks, the terrible, the tawny-eyed, the boon-giver, the kin of Yama, the one of unkempt hair, and to Śaṃkara of good conduct;
to the lovely one, the green-eyed, the Pillar, the Person, the golden-haired, the shaven, the lean, and the deliverer;
to the radiant as the sun, the holy ford, the God of gods, the swift, the one of many forms, Śarva, the beloved, the one in dear raiment;
to the turbaned, the fair-faced, the thousand-eyed, the bountiful, the dweller on the mountain, the wholly tranquil, the Lord clad in rags;
to the golden-armed, to the fierce, to the Lord of the quarters, to the Lord of the rain-cloud, and to the Lord of beings — homage!
To the Lord of trees, and likewise to the Lord of the waters, to the one whose body is veiled by trees, to the army's leader, to the one in the midst;
to the god with the sacrificial ladle in hand, the bowman, the kinsman of Bhṛgu, the one of many forms, the Lord of all, clad in rags;
to the thousand-headed, and to the thousand-eyed, to the thousand-armed, and to the thousand-footed.
Take refuge, O son of Kuntī, in the boon-giving Lord of the world, the lord of Umā, the strange-eyed, the destroyer of Dakṣa's sacrifice, the unperturbed Lord of creatures, the imperishable Lord of beings;
the braided-haired, whose course is the bull, whose navel is the bull, whose banner is the bull, the bull's own pride, the lord of the bull, the bull-horned, the bull among bulls;
the bull-marked, exalted as a bull, the bull itself, bull-eyed, whose weapon is the bull, whose arrow is the bull, who has become the bull, the Great Lord;

His spirit broken, sighing, wretched, crying "Alas, alas!" in deep anguish, Dhṛtarāṣṭra, the son of Ambikā, lamented, O great king.
Dhṛtarāṣṭra said: Saṃjaya, the heroic son of Adhiratha, of the prowess of a lion and an elephant, the bull among men of matchless shoulders, with the eyes, the gait, and the voice of a bull;
a bull who, like a bull, would not turn back in battle even from great Indra his foe — young, his frame hard as the thunderbolt;
he at the twang of whose bowstring and palm-guard and the roar of whose shower of arrows, chariots, horses, men, and elephants could not stand their ground in the fray;
relying upon whom — the mighty-armed, unfaltering destroyer of enemy hosts — Duryodhana waged his feud against the sons of Pāṇḍu of great strength:
how was that best of chariot-warriors, Karṇa, the tiger among men of irresistible prowess, slain by force in battle by Pārtha?
He who, ever relying on the might of his own arm, esteemed neither the unfaltering Kṛṣṇa nor Dhanaṃjaya, nor those other Vṛṣṇis;
who said, "The wielders of the Śārṅga and the Gāṇḍīva, united and unvanquished — I alone shall bring them down from their divine chariot in battle";
thus spoke he ever to that dullard, Duryodhana, deluded by greed, base, craving the kingdom, and distressed.
He who conquered foes most mighty and hard to subdue — the Gāndhāras, the Madrakas, the Matsyas, the Trigartas, the Taṅgaṇas, the Śakas;
the Pāñcālas and the Videhas, the Kuṇindas, the Kāśis and Kosalas, the Suhmas and the Aṅgas, the Puṇḍras, the Niṣādas, the Vaṅgas, and the Kīcakas;
the Vatsas, the Kaliṅgas, the Taralas, the Aśmakas, and the Ṛṣikas as well — that hero, having conquered them in battle, made them tribute-bearers of old.
As Uccaiḥśravas is the best of horses, as Vaiśravaṇa is the best of kings, as great Indra is the best of the gods, so Karṇa was the best of smiters.

But that Fate which moves of its own power protects the Pāṇḍavas and destroys us; for all those heroes who labored to achieve your purpose have been overpowered and slain by the enemy.
Heroes equal in might to Kubera, to Yama the son of Vivasvat, to Vāsava, and to Varuṇa the lord of waters—endowed with valor, with prowess, with strength, and with whole floods of manifold virtues—
those kings, well-nigh unslayable, who sought your welfare, have been slain in battle by the sons of Pāṇḍu. Grieve not for this, O Bhārata; this is destiny. Success comes by turns; it is not forever assured.
Hearing these words of the lord of the Madras, and surveying in his mind his own misconduct, Duryodhana, his spirit broken and his senses gone, sighed again and again, his form stricken with grief.
To him, mute with brooding, pitiable, sorely stricken and wretched, the son of Ārtāyana spoke these words: "Behold this dreadful battlefield, strewn with men, horses, and elephants, and with slain heroes.
With great elephants like mountains, fallen, flung down at a single stroke, their vital parts pierced by arrows—some of them writhing, others lifeless—their drivers, weapons, armor, and warriors all shattered;
like lordly mountains shattered by the thunderbolt, their rocks, beasts, trees, and herbs broken apart—their bells, goads, lances, and banners flung away, garlanded with gold and drenched in floods of blood;
with horses pierced by arrows and fallen, some still breathing, others vomiting blood, wretched and groaning, their eyes rolling, biting the earth and crying out piteously;
with elephant-warriors and horsemen flung down, some with breath ebbing, others lifeless—with men, horses, elephants, and chariots all crushed, the earth is grim to look upon, like the great Vaitaraṇī river of death.

Bows adorned with golden armlets and ornaments; arrows with shafts bright with gold; gleaming, spotless rapiers drawn from their scabbards; and javelins with swords, all shining like gold;
Parasols, yak-tail fans, conches, and garlands bright with the choicest flowers and gold; elephant-housings wrapped in banner-cloths; diadem-wreaths and gleaming crowns;
Whisks scattered about, and housings, and necklaces with quivering pendants of choicest pearls; head-ornaments, armlets, and fine bracelets; neck-chains and golden ornaments strung on threads of gold;
finest gems, diamonds, gold, and pearls, and auspicious jewels both great and small; bodies long accustomed to the utmost ease, and heads whose faces were like the moon;
Forsaking their bodies, their enjoyments, their possessions, and all that was delightful and pleasant, and attaining a great steadfastness in their own duty, they passed to the worlds that they had filled with their fame.
Having spoken thus, Śalya fell silent; and Duryodhana, his mind beset with grief, crying "Alas, Karṇa! Alas, Karṇa!", was stricken, bereft of his senses, his eyes streaming with tears.
All the kings, with the son of Droṇa at their head, comforted him and went on together, glancing again and again at the great banner of Arjuna, blazing with glory.
The earth, drenched red with the blood streaming from the bodies of men, horses, and elephants, shone like a woman robed and garlanded in red, gleaming with gold—open to the approach of all.
Their forms hidden by blood, O king, shining brightly in that dreadful hour, the Kurus could not stand their ground when they looked upon it; all were driven forth, as if banished from the worlds of the gods.
Sorely grieved by the slaying of Karṇa, crying "Alas, Karṇa! Alas, Karṇa!", they hastened swiftly to their camps, O king, gazing upon the reddened sun.

him who is hard to conceive even in mind by one of little understanding, hard to attain — I, even I, shall worship the destroyer of the Three Cities with the offering of my own self.
He who is praised, worthy of praise, being praised; the unfailing, the hide-clad, the blood-red, the blue-throated, the unmingled, the one who cannot be withstood;
the radiant, the maker of the universe, the Absolute, and the holy celibate; the keeper of vows, ever given to austerity, the endless one, the goal of those who practise penance;
the many-formed, the overlord of the hosts, the three-eyed, dear to his attendants; he upon whose face the chiefs of the hosts gaze, the beloved of Gaurī's heart;
the father of Kumāra, the tawny one, who rides the noblest of bulls; the scantily clad, the exceedingly fierce, intent upon the adorning of Umā;
the supreme, higher than the highest, the utmost, beyond whom nothing exists; the master of the finest of bows and weapons, and the very horizon of the south;
the golden-armoured god, adorned with the moon upon his crown — to that god I go for refuge, with the deepest concentration.
This dread peril too, so hard to cross, I shall cross this very day; with an offering of all beings, pure myself, I shall worship the Pure One.
Knowing the resolve of that great-souled one, his mind set upon self-surrender, a golden altar appeared before him.
On that altar, O king, the brilliant-rayed Fire then sprang up, filling heaven, the quarters and the intermediate quarters, and the sky with its flames.
And there appeared beings with blazing mouths and eyes, with many feet and heads and arms, vast as elephants and mountains, huge of countenance.
Some shaped like dogs, boars and camels; some with the faces of horses, jackals and cattle; some with the visages of bears and cats, and some with the muzzles of tigers and leopards;

some crow-mouthed, some frog-faced, and likewise parrot-beaked; some with the maws of great pythons, and some swan-faced and gleaming white;
some with the faces of woodpeckers and blue-jays, O Bhārata; some with the heads of turtles and crocodiles, and some with the snouts of porpoises;
some with the jaws of great sea-monsters, and likewise of whales; some lion-faced, some heron-mouthed, and some with the faces of doves and elephants;
some pigeon-faced, and likewise some with the bills of cormorants; some whose hands were ears, some thousand-eyed, and some with a hundred bellies;
some fleshless, some wolf-faced, and some hawk-visaged, O Bhārata; and some headless, O king, and some with the dread faces of bears;
some with eyes and tongues ablaze, and likewise some with flaming mouths; some ram-faced, and others again with the heads of goats, O lord of men;
some conch-hued, some conch-faced, and likewise some conch-eared; some girt with garlands of conches, their voices like the blast of conches;
some matted-haired, some with five tufts, and some shaven-headed and lean of belly; some four-tusked, four-tongued, with pointed ears, and some crowned with diadems;
some wearing chaplets, O lord of kings, and some with curling locks; some turbaned, some helmeted, fair of face and richly adorned;
some wearing wreaths of lotus and water-lily, and some bearing white lilies; and endowed with greatness, by hundreds and by thousands.
In their hands they bore the hundred-slaying engine and the discus, and clubs in their fists; some held the bhuśuṇḍi and the noose, and some maces, O Bhārata.
Quivers were bound upon their backs, their arrows of many hues, and they were furious for battle; with banners and pennons, with bells and battle-axes.
With great nooses raised in their hands, and some with cudgels in their grip; some bearing pillars, some swords, their diadems reared up like serpents; wearing armlets of mighty snakes, and decked in ornaments of varied splendour.

Bearing cold and wind and heat, enduring hunger and thirst and weariness, drying up this body by austerity such as the ordinances prescribe.
Ever hearing the cries, high and low, that delight the mind and ear, of the joyful beasts and birds that dwell in the woodlands.
Breathing in the delicate fragrances of the blossoming trees and creepers, beholding in the forest the lovely woodland dwellers of every form.
Even toward the forest-dwelling hermit who keeps to his lineage I shall do no unkindness when he comes into my sight; how much less, then, toward those who dwell in villages.
Devoted to solitude and given to reflection, I shall sustain myself on what is ripe or unripe, and gratify the ancestors and the gods with the produce of the wild, with sacred words, and with water.
Thus observing the severe—nay, the ever more severe—ordinance of the forest scriptures, I shall await the ending of this body.
Or else, alone and for a single day at the foot of each several tree, a shaven sage going about for alms, I shall wear away this frame.
Covered over with dust, taking shelter in an empty house, or making my dwelling at the root of a tree, having renounced all that is dear and all that is hateful;
neither grieving nor rejoicing, holding blame and self-praise alike, having become free of longing, free of possessiveness, beyond the pairs of opposites, and owning nothing;
taking delight in the Self, serene of spirit, in semblance like one dull, blind, and deaf, never forming any compact with another, with anyone at all;
doing no harm to any of the four kinds of beings, moving and unmoving, but even-minded toward all creatures, who abide each in its own nature, toward every bearer of life;
mocking no one, never knitting the brow in anger anywhere, ever serene of countenance, with all the senses well restrained;

asking no one the way, walking by whatever path it may be, desiring to go to no particular region and to no particular quarter;
indifferent as to my going, and never looking back behind me, walking straight onward with collected mind, sparing both the moving and the unmoving;
my own nature leading the way before me, my morsels of food springing up of themselves—giving no thought to all those contraries and opposing pairs;
food little or savorless, or, when a first round has yielded nothing, going on to other houses too for a gift; and failing of a gift, completing seven such rounds.
When the smoke has died, the pestle is laid by, the embers are cold, and the household has eaten, when the passing round of the begging-bowls is over and the hour of mendicants is gone;
going for alms but once a day, to two houses, or to five, freed of the snares of craving, I shall wander over this earth.
Acting in no way as one who longs to live, nor as one who longs to die, neither welcoming life and death nor loathing them;
toward the one who hews my arm with an adze and the one who anoints the other arm with sandal-paste, thinking neither ill nor good of either of the two;
having wholly abandoned all those acts of worldly advancement that a living man may perform, abiding within the span of a single instant and the like;
ever unattached to those acts, having forsaken all the workings of the senses, having well renounced all design, having cleansed away the defilement of the self;
released from every clinging, gone beyond all snares, standing in no one's power, sharing the nature of the wind that moves through the sky.
Wandering thus, free of passion, I shall attain to lasting contentment; for it is by craving, out of ignorance, that I have been made to commit great sin.
Having done deeds both good and evil, certain men maintain what they call their own kindred, bound together as effect with cause.

A guardian of the earth ought not to appoint a servant without testing him; the king who is thronged with men of low birth does not prosper in ease.
A man of good birth, appointed by the king, by reason of that good birth never sets his mind on wickedness, even when reproached though he be guiltless.
But a man of low birth, appointed through the ruin of worthier folk, having gained a lordship hard for him to win, if reproached will turn to enmity.
One well-born, trained, and wise, skilled in knowledge and discernment, knowing the true sense of all the treatises, enduring, and native to the land;
grateful and strong, patient, self-disciplined, master of his senses, free from greed, content with what he has, and eager for the good of his master's friends;
a minister who knows place and time, devoted to the gathering of all things needful, honoured, steady of mind, well-wishing, and never slothful;
of fitting conduct within his own domain, skilled in alliance and in war, knowing the threefold ends of the king, and beloved of townsfolk and countryfolk alike;
versed in the truth of entrenchments and battle-arrays, skilled in heartening the troops, knowing the meaning of gesture and demeanour, and expert in the march and the campaign;
learned in the training of elephants, free of arrogance, bold, deft, self-controlled, strong, and acting fittingly;
pure, surrounded by pure folk, well-attired and pleasing to behold, a leader skilled in statecraft, endowed with the sixty virtues—
unstubborn, humble, capable, soft of speech, steadfast, gentle, of great means, and able to provide for place and occasion—
the king who appoints such a minister and never slights him—his realm spreads wide as the moonlight of the lord of planets.
A king endowed with these very virtues, skilled in the treatises, supreme in dharma and intent on guarding his subjects, is one to be desired.

whatever weapons of war there are at all here, the Rod, being the soul of them all, moves embodied through the world.
Splitting, cleaving, crushing, slicing, rending, and tearing asunder, slaying and charging onward—the Rod alone moves abroad.
It is the Sword, the Slayer, Dharma, the Keen-pathed, the Unassailable, Womb-of-fortune, Victory, the Chastiser, Administration-of-justice, the Watcher-over-subjects;
the Scripture, the Brāhmaṇa's spell, the Chastiser who has become the prior word, Guardian-of-dharma, the Imperishable, the God, the Truth-faring, the Ever-faring, the Seizer;
the Unattached, son of Rudra, eldest of men, bringer of weal—these names of the Rod have been recounted, O Yudhiṣṭhira.
For the Rod is the Lord Viṣṇu, the Sacrifice, Nārāyaṇa the master; bearing forever its great form, it is called the Supreme Being.
As she is called the daughter of Brahmā—Lakṣmī, Polity, Sarasvatī—the science of governance by the Rod is the sustainer of the world; for the Rod is of many bodies.
Gain and loss, pleasure and pain, dharma and its opposite, strength and weakness, ill fortune and good fortune, merit and demerit, virtue and its absence,
desire and aversion, season and month, night and day and moment, displeasure and grace, joy, anger, calm and restraint,
fate and human effort, release and bondage, fear and fearlessness, harm and harmlessness, austerity, sacrifice, self-control, and poison and its absence,
the end, the beginning, and the middle, and the unfolding of all deeds; intoxication, negligence, and arrogance, hypocrisy, steadfastness, right conduct and wrong;
incapacity and capacity, pride and rigidity, expense and saving, discipline and emission, the timely and the untimely, O Bhārata;
falsehood, knowledge and ignorance, truth, and faith and faithlessness likewise, cowardice and resolve, gain and loss, victory and defeat;

Then, when they had heard that foremost word, pleasing to the mind and abounding in reason, those gathered there raised their voices and rejoiced, and folded their hands in homage to the hero of the Kurus.
Having heard that speech of Pārtha — adorned with fair letters, syllables, and sounds, pleasing to the mind, cleansed of every thorn of language — the lords of men praised it, O king. Then once more, undiminished in spirit, he questioned the son of the great river concerning the further dharma.
Yudhiṣṭhira said: O grandsire, deeply wise, increaser of the glory of the Kurus, I shall put a certain question; this you ought to expound to me.
What manner of men are agreeable? With whom does the highest affection arise? Who are fit to be trusted, both for the long term and the present moment? Of these, tell me.
For neither abundant wealth nor kinsmen and relations avail there where true friends stand fast — such is my conviction.
Hard to find is a friend who will listen, and hard to find a friend who seeks one's good. All this, O best of the upholders of dharma, you ought to explain.
Bhīṣma said: The men with whom alliance may be made, O king, and those with whom it may not — hear of them truly, in full, from me as I speak, O Yudhiṣṭhira.
The greedy, the cruel, the man who has abandoned dharma, the deceitful and the crooked, the base, the man of evil conduct, the suspicious of all, and the sluggish;
the procrastinator, the dishonest, the harsh, the violator of an elder's wife, he who forsakes a friend in distress, the wicked-souled and shameless;
he who sees evil on every side, the unbeliever and reviler of the Veda, and the man who roves the world with senses scattered, given over to lust;
the false, the hated of the world, the man who keeps no covenant, the slanderer, the untrained in wisdom, the envious, the fixed in evil purpose;

the ill-natured, the undisciplined in self, the savage, the gambler, and he who ever exploits his friends for gain, intent on profit alone;
the dull-witted man who is never content though one serves him to the utmost of one's power, and who feeds upon a friend as though upon a foe, O bull among men;
he who is angered without cause and grows estranged for no reason, and the sinner who swiftly casts off well-meaning friends;
the fool who takes offense at the slightest wrong, even at one done in ignorance, who courts his friends only when he has need of them yet hates them at heart, O lord of men;
and the enemy who wears a friend's face, the man of crooked glance, the seducer who takes no delight in what is good — such a man one should forsake.
The drunkard, the hateful, the cruel, the pitiless and harsh, the tormentor of others, the betrayer of friends, and he who delights in the slaying of living things;
the ingrate, basest in the world — with such a one alliance may in no way be made; nor with the man who hunts for another's weaknesses. Now hear from me those with whom alliance may be made.
Men of good birth, gifted in speech, skilled in knowledge and discernment, faithful to friends and grateful, all-knowing and free of grief;
endowed with the grace of sweetness, true to their pledges, masters of their senses, ever diligent in effort, who maintain their sons and dependents, sprung from noble line;
comely, possessed of virtues, free from greed, conquerors of fatigue, untouched by the well-known faults — these, indeed, a king should embrace.
For the good, O lord, are content with service rendered to the best of one's power; they are not wrathful out of season, nor estranged without cause.
Even when estranged they harbor no anger, not even in mind, those skilled in worldly affairs; bent on a friend's cause even at cost to themselves, they no more lose their bond with friends than dye loses its hold upon wool.

