The skin, declare those skilled in the discernment of the principles, is the inner principle; touch alone is the elemental aspect; and the Wind is the presiding deity.
The mind, they declare according to the teaching of scripture, is the inner principle; what is to be thought is the elemental aspect; and the Moon is the presiding deity.
That which springs from the I-sense, they say according to the teaching of the principles, is the inner principle; self-conceit is the elemental aspect; and Bhava is the presiding deity there.
The intellect, they say according to the teaching of the Veda, is the inner principle; what is to be known is the elemental aspect; and the Knower of the Field is the presiding deity here.
This manifest power has been described to you in due order, O king, at the beginning, the middle, and the end, in truth, by one who knows the truth.
Nature transforms the qualities by her own will, of her own desire, for the sake of play, O great king, by hundreds and by thousands.
As mortals kindle thousands of lamps from a single lamp, so Nature transforms the many qualities of the Person.
Goodness, delight, abundance, gladness, and luminosity; happiness, purity, health, contentment, and faithfulness;
freedom from meanness, absence of agitation, forbearance, firmness, harmlessness, evenness, truth, freedom from debt, gentleness, modesty, and steadiness;
purity, uprightness, good conduct, freedom from greed, absence of inner turmoil, and not boasting of deeds done amid the partings from the loved and the unloved;
showing favor through giving, working for another's good without craving gain, and compassion for all beings — these are remembered as the qualities of goodness.
The mass of the qualities of passion: form embodied in dominion, excessive eating, mercilessness, and the pursuit of pleasure and pain;

delight in reviling others and the practice of quarrels, egotism, discourtesy, anxiety, and the cultivation of enmity;
torment, plundering, loss of shame, and crookedness; division, harshness, lust and anger, intoxication, pride, hatred, and abusive speech — these are declared to be the qualities of passion.
Now I shall declare the mass of those belonging to darkness; let it be marked: delusion, obscurity, gloom, and that known as blinding gloom;
death is called blinding gloom, and anger is called gloom. Here are the marks of darkness: a craving for things to be eaten;
insatiability in food and likewise no satisfaction in drink; indulgence in perfumes and garments, in amusements, in beds and seats;
delight in sleeping by day, in dispute, and in heedlessness; faith placed in dancing, instruments, and song through ignorance; and hatred of the particular duties of dharma — these are the qualities of darkness.
Yājñavalkya said: These three are the qualities of Primal Nature, O best of men; and they abide ever inseparable from the whole world.
A hundredfold, a thousandfold, and likewise a hundred-thousandfold, and by tens of millions, this one makes the indwelling self by the self.
The contemplators of the inner self declare that for the man of goodness the highest station, for the man of passion the middle, and for the man of darkness the lowest station.
By merit alone here one attains the upward course; by merit mixed with sin, the human state; and by unrighteousness, the downward course.
Now hear from me in truth the pairing of these three and their commingling — of goodness, of passion, and of darkness.
In goodness passion is seen, and in passion likewise darkness; in darkness, too, goodness; and in goodness, the Unmanifest itself.
He who is joined with the Unmanifest and goodness attains the world of the gods; he who is joined with passion and goodness is born among men.

He who is the Absolute even of the absolutes, the supreme even of the supreme, the radiance even of radiances, the austerity even of austerities;
The peace even of those at peace, the brilliance even of the brilliant, the self-controlled even among the self-controlled, the wisdom even of the wise;
The god even of the gods, the sage even of the sages, the sacrifice even of sacrifices, the auspicious even of auspicious things;
The Rudra even of the Rudras, the lord even of the mighty, the yogin even of yogins, and the cause of all causes;
From whom the worlds arise and into whom they cease to be — of Hara, who has become the Self of all beings, of measureless splendor.
Now hear from me the thousand-and-eight names of Śarva, on hearing which, O best of men, you shall obtain all your desires.
The Steadfast, the Pillar, the Lord, the Radiant, the Most Excellent, the Boon-giver, the Best; the Self of all, the All-renowned, the All, the Maker of all, Bhava the Existent.
The Matted-haired, the Hide-clad, the Crested; the All-bodied, the Source of all becoming; Hari, the Doe-eyed, the Lord who withdraws all beings.
Engagement and Disengagement; the Constant, the Eternal, the Fixed; the Haunter of cremation grounds, the Blessed One, the Sky-rover, the Earth-rover, the Tormentor.
The One to be saluted, of mighty deeds, the Ascetic, the Nourisher of beings; Hidden in the guise of a madman, the Lord of all creatures in every world.
Of great form, of mighty frame, of every form, of great renown; the Great-souled, the All-pervading, the Misshapen, the Dwarf, the Man.
The Guardian of the world, the Hidden-souled, Serene Grace, He of horse and ass; the Pure, the Mighty, Discipline itself, and the Refuge of discipline.
The Doer of all deeds, the Self-existent, the Beginning, the First Maker, the Treasure-store; the Thousand-eyed, the Strange-eyed, Soma, the Marshaller of the stars.

Maheśvara said: Hear from me, with mind composed, the dharma that belongs to the forest-hermits; and having heard it single-mindedly, O goddess, let your thought be set wholly upon dharma.
By the accomplished, the disciplined, the good who have taken up dwelling in the forest—by such forest-hermits this rite is to be performed; hear of what kind it is.
Bathing thrice at the three times of day, the rite of worshipping ancestors and gods, diligent tending of the fire-offering, and the procedure of sacrifice and oblation;
the gathering of wild rice, the eating of fruits and roots, and the use of oils pressed from the seeds of the iṅgudī for their unctuous nourishment;
by the perfected who follow the practice of yoga, the casting off of desire and anger; by those who keep the hero's couch and resort to the hero's posture;
by the steadfast yoked in yoga, the good, who in summer endure the five fires, who keep the frog-posture of yoga and observe each rule according to the ordinance;
by those ever seated in the hero's pose and sleeping upon the bare ground, the discipline of cold and the discipline of fire are to be practiced by minds bent upon dharma.
By those who feed on water and on air, whose chief food is moss; by the self-restrained who pound their grain with stones, and by others who wash and cleanse their food;
by those clad in bark-cloth and wearing the skins of deer—the round of life is to be carried out according to the season, according to dharma, according to the ordinance.
By those constant to the forest, who roam the forest, who guard it and range within it, the forest is to be approached as though it were a guru, and there the forest-dwellers must abide.
For them the rite of oblation is dharma, the observance of the five great sacrifices, and the keeping of the Veda-ordained sacrifice on the fifth day sacred to the serpents.

